1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
 The title of this chapter is Replacement and Retention Decisions.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:18,000
 We are studying a specific type of investment problems, namely should we retain the current machine or should we replace it by a newer version.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
 In this chapter we will not see new criteria to evaluate investments.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:31,000
 We already saw the annual, present and future world. Last time we saw the rate of return.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
 Here we will use the annual world, but for a specific type of problems.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,000
 This is an overview, it's a rather short chapter.

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
 What we also want to do is this.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:59,000
 If you google or you look in Science Direct and you look replacement problems, you will find a very specific type of model.

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:09,000
 Let's see whether these models are completely in line with what our engineering economy handbook says about replacement problems or not.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:17,000
 Why would we consider replacing our current machine installation plant?

11
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 Because it shows reduced performance, wear and tear. It's not so reliable anymore, it's not productive enough anymore.

12
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 Operating costs, for instance energy consumption is increasing.

13
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 There are different requirements. New production is needed, more accurate production is needed or the asset that we have is become...

14
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:52,000
 Well, we can't use it anymore if we want to be competitive because for instance the technology is not state of the art anymore.

15
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,000
 So we cannot be competitive if we stick with it.

16
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:06,000
 Here you see, we brought together some terminology. I think most of the terminology you know.

17
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
 Let me just point out the new things to you.

18
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,000
 We have our old installation that we are considering for replacement.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,000
 Well, that old installation is called the defender asset. The one we want to replace it with is called the challenger.

20
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
 Definitely two mutually exclusive options. We will not keep the old one and buy a new one.

21
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
 So it's either one thing or another.

22
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
 Another thing that is important, the notion of economic service life.

23
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 We will see it in more detail later on.

24
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,000
 But basically it's the number of years at which the lowest annual worth of costs occurs.

25
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 We will encounter market value for the defender.

26
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 That's a real current fair market value.

27
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 So it's really what the market is willing to pay for it.

28
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:12,000
 It's not what you think that it could be or it's also not what your book value says.

29
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:19,000
 Very very important in this chapter is that we take the consultant's point of view.

30
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:32,000
 Basically what it means is that you take the point of view of an outsider.

31
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:42,000
 And that means that you also take into account that keeping the defender is costing money.

32
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:52,000
 Because you give up the opportunity to receive a possible cash flow from the current defender.

33
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:59,000
 So that's what is important. The defender also still has in our formulas an initial investment.

34
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
 But I think it will become more clear when we see some examples.

35
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
 So this is a case study about the Arkansas division of a company called ADM.

36
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:19,000
 Three years ago they purchased a ground leveling system for rice field preparation at a cost of 120,000.

37
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:27,000
 At the time they expected the service life to be 10 years and after that a salvage value of 25,000 could still be received.

38
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
 An annual operating cost of 30,000 was expected.

39
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
 The current account book value is 80,000.

40
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:42,000
 The system is deteriorating rapidly and they expected after three more years of use the salvage value could be 10,000.

41
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
 Annual operating costs are still expected to be the 30,000.

42
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
 Now a new system has appeared on the market.

43
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:59,000
 Value of 100,000 dollar. Trading value for the current system today is 70,000.

44
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:05,000
 The price for that new system goes up next week to 110,000.

45
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
 But the trading value for our old one is still 70,000.

46
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:16,000
 The engineer estimates that the system would have a useful life of 10 years and a salvage value of 20 after that.

47
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
 Annual operating costs are estimated to be 20,000.

48
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
 So a lot less than, a bit less of our current system.

49
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 And a 70 market value appraisal of the current system was made today.

50
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
 Later on we will see that the timing of these estimates is important.

51
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:44,000
 So if no further analysis is made on the estimates, what are the correct values that we should use in our replacement study today?

52
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
 Ok, let's have a look.

53
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
 So this is the situation.

54
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:57,000
 Three years ago we bought this system, a ground leveling system.

55
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:02,000
 So this was the purchase price, the expected service life at the time was 10 years.

56
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:07,000
 Expected service value 25,000, annual operating cost 30,000.

57
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
 Now if you look today, its current book value is 80,000.

58
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,000
 We expect the life to be another three years.

59
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:23,000
 So if you see, well, three years ago it was 10 years so we would expect it to be seven years.

60
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
 But well, no, it's only three years.

61
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:32,000
 Expected salvage value, annual operating costs and market value appraisal.

62
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:38,000
 Ok, now what about the new system?

63
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
 Well, this is the data we obtained.

64
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
 Ok, now what is important for our replacement study?

65
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
 I see here a lot of numbers.

66
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:50,000
 Well, we have to take that consultant's view, outsider viewpoint.

67
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
 And then these are things that actually matter.

68
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:59,000
 What matters is our market value, current market value of the old system.

69
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:04,000
 Which we will be treating as an initial investment in our calculations.

70
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:09,000
 We have an annual operating cost, we have a salvage value and we have a life of three years.

71
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:17,000
 Here we have our initial investment, annual operating cost, salvage value and we have our life.

72
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:18,000
 What does it mean?

73
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:25,000
 It means that these things that you see that are, so here, maybe a bit confusing.

74
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:32,000
 So all these values are not important in our study anymore, in our replacement study.

75
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,000
 We don't need them, we are not interested in them anymore.

76
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:48,000
 So this is a summary of what we have been saying.

77
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
 So you must assign an investment cost for keeping the defender asset.

78
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:57,000
 That's the consequence of that consulting viewpoint that we take.

79
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,000
 We have to, a current marketplace value and of course the challenger.

80
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:11,000
 Well, it's just the numbers that we have for that new machine or equipment or whatever.

81
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:14,000
 That we want to replace.

